== Chapter XVI

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The strength of indwelling sin shown from its power and effects in unregenerate persons.]
]

#smallcaps[It] is the power and efficacy of #emph[indwelling sin,] as it remains in various degrees in believers, that we are treating of. Now I have shown elsewhere that its nature and all its natural properties still remain in them. So although we cannot prove directly what the #emph[strength] of sin is in believers from what its #emph[power] is in those in whom it is only checked and not weakened at all, still, by observing that power, we may warn believers of the real power of the #emph[deadly enemy] they have to deal with.

Suppose the plague is raging violently in #emph[one] city, destroying multitudes. And suppose there is in #emph[another] city an infection of the same kind, which does not rise to that height and fury there because of the #emph[check] it meets from a #emph[better air] and from the remedies used. A man may still show the inhabitants of the second city the force and danger of the #emph[infection] that has got in among them, by the #emph[effects] it has produced and is producing among others who do not have the benefit of the #emph[preventives] and preservatives they enjoy. This will both teach them to value the means of their preservation and make them more watchful against the power of the infection that is among them. So it is in this case. Believers may be taught what the power and efficacy of the #emph[plague of sin] in them and among them is, by the #emph[effects] the same plague produces in and among others, who do not have the #emph[checks] on its poison and the #emph[preservatives] from death with which the Lord Jesus has furnished believers.

Having fixed, then, on the demonstration of the power of sin from the #emph[effects] it produces, and having given a twofold instance of this in #emph[believers] themselves, I will now give further evidence of the same truth, or pursue the same evidence of it, by showing something of the power it exercises in those who are #emph[unregenerate,] and so do not have the remedies against it that believers are furnished with.

I will not handle the #emph[whole power of sin] in unregenerate persons. That is a very large field, and not the business I have in hand. I will only point out to believers, as I said, what they have to deal with, by a #emph[few instances] of its effects in the unregenerate.

#emph[First,] then, it appears in the #emph[violence] it does to the nature of man, compelling men to sins wholly contrary to all the principles of the rational nature God has given them. Every creature of God has had a #emph[law of operation] implanted in it at its creation. That law is the rule of everything that proceeds from it, of all it does of its own accord. So fire rises upward, weighty and heavy bodies fall downward, and water flows, each according to the principles of its nature, which give it the #emph[law of its operation.] Whatever hinders them in their operation is force and violence, as what hinders a #emph[stone] from falling or #emph[fire] from rising. What forces them to move contrary to the law of their nature, as a stone to go upward or fire to go downward, is the greatest #emph[violence] of its kind, and its degrees are endless. Now everyone would acknowledge that whatever could take a great #emph[millstone] and fling it upward into the air would be a thing of wonderful force, power, and efficacy.

Man also has his #emph[law of operation] and working created together with him. This may be considered in two ways: first, as it is #emph[common] to him and other creatures; or, secondly, as it is #emph[peculiar] to him, with reference to the special end for which he was made. Some things in this #emph[law of nature,] I say, #emph[are common to man and other creatures]: to nourish their young; to live quietly with those of the same kind and race; to seek and pursue what is good for them in the state and condition in which they were created. All #emph[brute living creatures] have these things in the law of their nature, as man also has.

But besides these things, man was created in a #emph[special way] to give glory to God by rational and moral obedience, and so to obtain a reward in the enjoyment of him. So there are many things in the #emph[law of his creation] that are peculiar to him: to #emph[love God above all,] to seek the enjoyment of him as his chief good and last end, to inquire after his mind and will, to yield obedience, and the like. All of these are part of the law of his nature.

Now these things are not distinguished as though a man might perform the actions of the law of his nature that are common to him and other creatures merely from the #emph[principles of his nature,] as they do. The #emph[law of his dependence on God,] and of doing everything in obedience to him, extends over all of them as well. He can never be considered a #emph[mere creature,] but a creature made for the glory of God by rational, moral obedience: #emph[rational,] because he chooses it and performs it with reason; and #emph[moral,] because it is regulated by a #emph[law] that reason attends to.

For example, man shares with other creatures the #emph[care for the nourishing of his children,] the young and helpless ones who receive their being through him. A love and care for them is implanted in him, in the principles of his nature created together with him; so it is with other living creatures. Now let other creatures answer this #emph[instinct and inclination,] and not be hardened against their young like the #emph[foolish ostrich,] to whom God has not given this natural wisdom (Job 39:16, 17), and they fully answer the #emph[law of their creation.] It is not so with man. It is not enough for him to answer the #emph[instinct] and secret impulse and inclination of his nature and kind, as in nourishing his children. He must also do it in #emph[subjection to God,] obeying him in it and doing it for his glory; for the law of #emph[moral obedience] extends over his whole being and all his operations. Indeed, the #emph[whole] of a man lies, as it were, in these things: in the things implanted in his nature as a creature, common to him and all other living creatures, backed by the command or will of God, since he is a creature capable of #emph[yielding moral obedience] and of doing everything for God’s glory.

Whatever, then, can drive and compel a man to transgress this #emph[law of his nature] must be reckoned of great force and efficacy. It is not only like #emph[throwing millstones upward,] or driving beasts from caring for their young, or taking from cattle of one kind their #emph[herding together] in quietness. Beyond that, it makes a man cast off, as far as lies in him, his #emph[fundamental dependence on God] as a creature made to obey him.

Now this is often done by #emph[indwelling sin] in #emph[unregenerate] persons. Let us take a few instances.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, there is nothing more deeply set into the principles of the nature of all living creatures, and so of man himself, than love for their young and care for their preservation and nourishment. Many brute creatures will #emph[die] for them; some feed them with their own flesh and blood. All deprive themselves of the #emph[food] that nature directs them to as their best, in order to #emph[give] it to them, and they act on their behalf to the utmost of their power.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now such is the efficacy, power, and force of #emph[indwelling sin] in man, an #emph[infection] the nature of other creatures knows nothing of, that in many it prevails to stop up this fountain. It beats back the stream of natural affections, roots up the principles of the law of nature, and drives men to #emph[neglect] and destroy the #emph[fruit of their own bodies. Paul] tells us that the Gentiles of old were ἄστοργοι, “without natural affection” (Rom 1:31). What he has in view is the barbarous custom among the #emph[Romans,] who often, to spare themselves the trouble of bringing up their children and to be free to satisfy their lusts, #emph[destroyed their own children from the womb.] So far did the strength of sin prevail to blot out the law of nature and to beat back its force and power.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Examples of this kind are common in all nations; among ourselves, of #emph[women murdering their own children] through the deceitful reasoning of sin. In this sin turns back the strong #emph[current] of nature, darkens all the #emph[light] of God in the soul, and overrules all natural #emph[principles,] even though they are backed by the power of the command and will of God. Yet this #emph[evil] has received a fearful aggravation through the efficacy of sin. Men have not only #emph[killed] their children, but cruelly #emph[sacrificed] them to satisfy their lusts. The apostle counts #emph[idolatry,] and so consequently all #emph[superstition,] among the works of the flesh (Gal 5:20); that is, among the fruit and product of #emph[indwelling sin.] It is from this that men have done the horrid and unspeakable violence to the law of nature just mentioned. So the psalmist tells us (Ps 106:37, 38). The same is mentioned again in Jer 7:31 and in various other places. I have described elsewhere the whole manner of that abomination. For the present it may be enough to point out that they took their children and #emph[burned them to ashes] in a slow fire. The wicked #emph[priests] who assisted at the sacrifice gave them this relief: they made a noise and clamor, so that the vile wretches might not hear the woeful moans and cries of the #emph[poor, dying, tormented infants.] I suppose that in this case we need no further evidence. #emph[Naturalists] can give no rational account of the secret force of that #emph[little fish] which, they say, will stop a ship in full sail in the middle of the sea; they can only wonder at it. And we must acknowledge that it is beyond our power to give an account of the #emph[secret force] and unsearchable deceit in this #emph[inbred] traitor, sin. It can not only stop the course of nature when all its #emph[sails] that carry it forward are filled, as they are in the affection for children. It can also #emph[drive it backward] with such violence and force as to make men deal with their #emph[own children] as a good man could not be hired by any reward to deal with his #emph[dog.] And it may not be to the disadvantage of the #emph[best] to know and consider that they carry about with them, and in them, the thing that has produced these effects in others.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The same may be said of all the other sins against the #emph[first dictates] of the law of nature with which mankind is or has been stained and disgraced: #emph[murder] of #emph[parents] and children, of wives and husbands, #emph[sodomy, incest,] and similar enormities. In all of them sin prevails in men against the #emph[whole law of their being] and dependence on God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Why should I count up the murders of #emph[Cain] and #emph[Abel,] or the treason of #emph[Judas,] with their aggravations? Or recall the filth and villainy of #emph[Nero,] in whom sin seemed to intend an instance of how far it could #emph[debase the nature] of man? In a word, all the studied, premeditated #emph[perjuries]\; all the planned, bloody #emph[revenges]\; all the filth and uncleanness; all the enmity against God and his ways that is in the world, is fruit growing from this root alone.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, sin shows its efficacy in keeping men from #emph[believing] under the dispensation of the gospel. This evidence must be cleared up a little further.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, under the dispensation of the gospel there are #emph[only a few who believe.] So its preachers complain (Isa 53:1): “Who has believed our message?” The apostle interprets this of the #emph[small number] of believers (John 12:38). Our Savior Christ himself tells us that “many are called”—the word is preached to many—but “few are chosen.” And so the church complains of its numbers (Mic 6:1). There are #emph[few] who enter by the #emph[narrow gate,] and daily experience confirms this woeful observation. How many villages, parishes, and even towns may we go to where the gospel has perhaps been preached for many years, and perhaps hardly meet one #emph[true believer] in them, one who shows forth the death of Christ in his way of life! In the best places, those most eminent for profession, are not such persons like the berries left when an olive tree is beaten, “two or three berries atop the tree, four or five on its highest branches”?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, in the preaching of the gospel everything that #emph[separately] prevails on men to do whatever else they do in their lives is set before them together, as #emph[motives to believing.] Whatever anyone does with deliberation, he does either because it is #emph[reasonable] and #emph[good] for him to do it, or because it is #emph[profitable] and advantageous, or #emph[pleasant,] or lastly #emph[necessary] for avoiding evil. Whatever men do with deliberation, I say, whether it is good or evil, whether in the works of this life or in things that lead to another, they do it from one or another of the reasons or motives mentioned. And, God knows, the motives men are prevailed on by are often very #emph[poor] and mean of their kind. How often will men be led, for a very #emph[little pleasure] or a very #emph[little profit,] to do what will embitter their lives and damn their souls! And what effort they will make to avoid what they take to be #emph[evil] or grievous to them! Any one of these is enough to #emph[oil the wheels] of men’s utmost efforts and set them to work in earnest.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But now all these things meet in the offer of the gospel and the #emph[command to believe]\; and each of them #emph[in such a way] that the whole world can offer nothing like it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It is the most #emph[reasonable thing] that can be put to a man’s understanding. Through his #emph[own fault] he has lost the #emph[way] in which he was first placed, the way of bringing glory to God and saving his own soul, the ends for which he was made. It is most reasonable that he should accept and embrace that other blessed, easy, safe, excellent #emph[way] of attaining those ends, which God in infinite grace, love, mercy, wisdom, and righteousness has found out and offers to him. And,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] it is the #emph[most profitable] thing a man can possibly be invited to, if there is any #emph[profit,] benefit, or advantage in the #emph[forgiveness of sins,] in the love and favor of God, in a blessed immortality, in eternal glory. And,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Thirdly,] it is most #emph[pleasant] as well. Surely it is a pleasant thing to be brought out of #emph[darkness into light,] out of a dungeon to a throne, from captivity and slavery to Satan and cursed lusts into the glorious liberty of the children of God, with a thousand heavenly #emph[sweetnesses] not to be mentioned now. And,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Fourthly,] it is surely #emph[necessary.] It is necessary not only from the #emph[command of God,] who has supreme authority over us, but also indispensably so for avoiding the #emph[eternal ruin] of body and soul (Matt 16:16). It is constantly offered on these terms: believe, or you perish under the weight of the wrath of the great God, and that forever.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But in spite of the fact that all these considerations are #emph[preached] to men, and pressed on them in the name of the great God, from day to day and from one year to another, yet, as was observed before, there are very #emph[few] who set their hearts on them so as to embrace what they lead to. Tell men #emph[ten thousand times] that this is wisdom, and indeed riches; that all their profit lies in it; that they will assuredly and eternally perish, perhaps within a few hours, if they do not receive the gospel. Assure them that it is their #emph[only interest] and concern. Let them know that God himself says all this to them. Still it is all one: they do not regard it, they do not set their hearts on it, but as good as say plainly, “We will have nothing to do with these things.” They will rather perish in their lusts than accept mercy.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, it is #emph[indwelling sin] that both disables men from believing and hinders them from it, and that alone. #emph[Blindness] of mind, stubbornness of will, and sensuality of the affections all join to keep poor perishing souls at a distance from Christ. Sin makes men #emph[blind,] so that they cannot see his excellencies; #emph[obstinate,] so that they will not lay hold of his righteousness; #emph[senseless,] so that they take no notice of their own eternal concerns.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now certainly whatever can prevail on men who are wise, sober, and prudent in other things to neglect and despise the love of God, the blood of Christ, and the eternal welfare of their own souls, on weak and worthless pretexts, must be acknowledged to have an astonishing force and efficacy going with it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Whose heart, if he has once heard of the ways of God, can help bleeding to see #emph[poor souls] eternally perishing under a #emph[thousand gracious invitations] to accept mercy and pardon in the blood of Christ? And can we help being astonished at the power of the principle that makes them run headlong to their own destruction? Yet all this comes upon them from the power and deceit of the #emph[sin] that dwells in them.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. It is evident in their #emph[total apostasies.] Many men who are not really converted are much worked on by the word. The apostle tells us that they “truly escape from those who live in error” (2 Pet 2:18). They separate themselves from idolatry and false worship, owning and professing the truth. They also “escape the corruption of the world” (v. 20); that is, “the corruption in the world caused by lust,” as he puts it in ch. 1:4, the filthy, corrupt, and unclean ways that the men of the world walk and live in as they pursue their lusts. These they #emph[escape] by amending their lives and ordering their conduct according to the convictions they have from the word. For he tells us that all this is brought about “through the knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ”\; that is, by the preaching of the gospel. They are worked on so far as #emph[to forsake] all ways of false worship, to #emph[profess] the truth, to #emph[reform] their lives, and to walk according to the convictions that are on them.]

By this means they gain the reputation of professors. “They have a reputation for being alive” (Rev 3:1), and they share in some or all of the #emph[privileges] of the gospel that the apostle lists in Heb 6:4, 5.

It is not my present business to show #emph[how far,] or in what respects, a man may be #emph[effectually worked] on by the word and yet not be #emph[really won] over to close with Christ; or what the utmost bounds and limits of a #emph[common work] of grace on unregenerate men may be. It is confessed on all sides that it may be carried so far that it is very difficult to tell its effects and products apart from those of the grace that is special and saving.

But in spite of all this, we see many of these men daily #emph[fall away from God,] utterly and wickedly: some into #emph[debauchery] and uncleanness, some into #emph[worldliness] and covetousness, some into #emph[persecuting] the saints, all to the perdition of their own souls. The apostle declares in the place mentioned how this comes about. They are, he says, #emph[entangled] again. To #emph[entice and entangle] is the proper work of #emph[indwelling sin,] as I have shown before from Jas 1:14, 15. It alone #emph[entangles] the soul. As the apostle says in #emph[v.] 18, they are “enticed from their whole profession” into cursed apostasy through the lusts of the flesh.

Through its deceit and power it prevails on them to #emph[abandon their profession] utterly, and their whole engagement to God. And this proves the greatness of its strength and efficacy in several ways.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. It does so in that it checks and overrules the #emph[exceeding greatness of power] put forth in the word in their conviction and reformation. We see by experience that men are not #emph[easily worked] on by the word. Most men can live under its ministry all the days of their lives and remain as senseless and dull as the #emph[seats] they sit on, or the flint in the rock. Mighty difficulties and prejudices must be conquered, and great #emph[blows] must be given to the conscience, before this can be brought about. It is like stopping a #emph[river] in its course and turning its streams another way; or keeping a #emph[stone] from falling downward; or turning back the #emph[wild donkey] when she is furiously set on going her way, as the prophet speaks (Jer 2:24). It takes mighty power and strength to turn men from their corrupt ways, sins, and pleasures; to make them #emph[pray, fast, hear,] and do many things contrary to the principle of the flesh that secretly predominates in them, and to do them willingly and gladly; to make them #emph[profess Christ] and the gospel, perhaps under some trials and reproaches; to give them #emph[light] to see into various mysteries, and #emph[gifts] for carrying out various duties; to make #emph[dead,] blind, senseless men #emph[walk] and #emph[talk] and do all the outward offices and duties of living and healthy men; along with similar accompaniments of conviction and reformation. These are its effects and products. Indeed, the power the Holy Spirit puts forth by the word is inexpressible: in staggering and #emph[convicting] sinners, in #emph[awakening] their consciences, #emph[enlightening] their minds, #emph[changing] their affections, #emph[awing] their hearts, #emph[reforming] their #emph[lives,] and #emph[compelling] them to duties.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But now to all of these #emph[indwelling sin] gives a #emph[check] and overrules them. It prevails against this #emph[whole work] of the Spirit by the word, with all the advantages of providential dealings, in afflictions and mercies, that go with it. Once sin is enraged, all these things become like the fresh bowstrings and ropes #emph[Samson] was bound with before his head was shaved. Only cry out to it, “The #emph[Philistines] are here! Here is a #emph[subtle, a suitable temptation;] now show your strength and efficacy,” and all these things become “like a strand of yarn seared by a flame.” #emph[Conscience] is stifled, #emph[reputation] in the church of God despised, #emph[light] overthrown, the #emph[impressions] of the word thrown off, #emph[convictions] swallowed down, heaven and hell despised. Sin makes its way through everything, and turns the soul utterly from the good and right ways of God. Sometimes it does this subtly, by #emph[imperceptible degrees.] It takes away all the force of former impressions from the Spirit by the word; it stains conscience by degrees, hardens the heart, and makes the affections sensual by its various workings. So the poor “backslider in heart” hardly knows what he is doing until he has come to the very bottom of all impiety, profaneness, and enmity against God. Sometimes, joining with some #emph[vigorous temptation,] it suddenly and all at once plunges the soul into a course of estrangement from God and from the profession of his ways.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. It takes them away from the #emph[hopes of heaven] they had attained through their convictions, obedience, and #emph[temporary faith] or believing. There is a #emph[general hope of heaven,] or at least of escaping hell, of an #emph[untroubled immortality,] in the most dull and stupid souls in the world, who are persuaded, by tradition or by instruction from the word, that there is another state of things to come after this life. But in #emph[unconvinced,] unenlightened persons it is a dull, senseless, unmoving thing. It has no other hold on them, and no power in them, except to keep them free from the trouble and perplexity of contrary thoughts and fears. It is otherwise with those who are worked on by the word as we have declared before. Their #emph[hope of heaven] and a blessed immortality is often accompanied with great #emph[joys] and exultations, and is a relief to them under and against the worst of their fears and trials. It is such that they would not #emph[part with] it for all the world; and on every occasion they retreat to it in their minds for comfort and relief.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now by the #emph[power of sin] they are prevailed on to give up all this. “Let heaven go if it will, a blessed immortality with the enjoyment of God himself; sin must be served, and provision made to fulfill its lusts.”]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Suppose a man in the things of this world had such a hope of a large #emph[inheritance,] of a kingdom, that he was satisfied it would not fail him, but that in the end he would surely enjoy it, and lead a happy and glorious life in possession of it for many days. And suppose someone went to him and said, “It is true, the #emph[kingdom] you look for is a wide and honorable dominion, full of all good and desirable things, and you may attain it. But come, throw away all hopes and expectations of it, and join me in the #emph[service and slavery] of this or that oppressive tyrant.” You will readily grant that he must have some strange, bewitching power about him if he could prevail on a man in his right mind to follow his advice.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Yet so it is, and much more so, in the case we have in hand. Sin itself cannot deny that the #emph[kingdom of heaven] the soul hopes for and expects is glorious and excellent. Nor does it set about convincing the man that his thoughts of it are #emph[vain] and will deceive him. It simply prevails on him to throw away his #emph[hopes] and despise the #emph[kingdom] he was expecting, on no other motive than that he may serve some #emph[worldly, cruel,] or filthy and sensual lust. Certainly here lies a secret efficacy whose depths cannot be fathomed.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. The apostle shows the power of the entanglements of sin in and on #emph[apostates] in this: that it turns them away from “the way of righteousness after they have known it” (2 Pet 2:21). At the last day it will be found “evil and bitter” that men live all their days in the service of sin, self, and the world, refusing to make any #emph[trial] of the #emph[ways of God] they are invited to. They have no experience of the excellence, beauty, pleasantness, and safety of those ways; yet they have had evidence brought to them from God himself that the ways are such, and so refusing them will, I say, be #emph[bitterness] in the end. But the condition of those is far worse still who, as the apostle says, have “known the way of righteousness” and are turned aside from the holy commandment by the power of #emph[indwelling sin.] To leave God for the devil after a man has made some trial of #emph[him] and his service; heaven for hell after a man has had some cheering, refreshing thoughts of it; the fellowship of the saints for an #emph[alehouse] or a #emph[brothel] after a man has been admitted to their communion and tasted its pleasantness; to leave walking in pure, clear, straight paths in order to #emph[wallow in mire,] sewage, and filth: this will be cause for #emph[lamentation.] Yet sin prevails on #emph[apostates] to do this. It does so against all their light, conviction, experiences, professions, and engagements, and whatever else may be #emph[strong] on them to keep them in the known ways of righteousness.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. It proves its strength in them by prevailing on them to #emph[a total renunciation of God] as revealed in Christ, and of the power of all gospel truth, #emph[in the sin against the Holy Spirit.] I do not now determine precisely what the #emph[sin against the Holy Spirit] is, or what it consists in. There are different views of it. All agree in this: that by it an end is put to #emph[all dealings between God and man in a way of grace.] It is a sin that leads to death. And the hardness and blindness of many men’s hearts bring them to this; by these they are at length set beyond the reach of mercy. #emph[They choose to have nothing more to do with God,] and God swears that they will never enter his rest. So “sin gives birth to death.” By it a man is brought to renounce the end for which he was made, to reject #emph[willfully] the means by which he might come to the enjoyment of God, to provoke God to his face, and so to perish in his rebellion.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I have not mentioned these things as though I hoped to set out by them #emph[the full power of indwelling sin in unregenerate men.] I only intended to give a glimpse of it by a few instances. Anyone who wants a fuller view of it need only #emph[open his eyes] and take a little look at the wickedness that #emph[reigns,] indeed #emph[rages,] all over the world. Let him consider the prevailing flood of the things #emph[Paul] names as “the acts of the flesh” (Gal 5:19, 20, 21) among the sons of men, in all places, nations, cities, towns, and parishes. Then let him add to this just one consideration. As to the outward acting of these abominations, the world, which is full of their steam, filth, and blood, is a #emph[pleasant garden,] a #emph[paradise,] compared with the heart of man. In that heart they are all conceived, and hourly #emph[millions] more of viler abominations, which are stifled in the womb by some of the ways dwelt on before, and so can never be brought forth into the light. Let a man take this course, I say, using the law as his light and rule, and if he has any spiritual discernment he may quickly be satisfied in this matter.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And I showed at the beginning of this discourse how this consideration fully confirms the truth proposed.]]
